1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,353
Welcome to Jerry Documentaries.

2
00:00:02,353 --> 00:00:11,800
In this episode we follow Ibn Battuta, a young man from Tangier who started a pilgrimage and ended up traveling for almost thirty years.

3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:20,709
Ibn Battuta was born in Tangier, in Morocco, in the year thirteen hundred and four, into a family of scholars.

4
00:00:20,709 --> 00:00:28,600
He studied Islamic law and grew up in a city that was a meeting place of sailors, merchants and ideas.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:36,131
In thirteen twenty five, at about twenty one years old, he left Tangier to make the pilgrimage to Mecca.

6
00:00:36,131 --> 00:00:43,400
He crossed North Africa, sometimes alone and sometimes with caravans, and reached Egypt, Syria and Arabia.

7
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:46,327
After the pilgrimage he kept going.

8
00:00:46,327 --> 00:00:58,600
He visited Persia and Iraq, then Anatolia and Central Asia, and arrived in India, where he spent several years serving at the court of a sultan before continuing his way.

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,900
He sailed to the Maldives and Southeast Asia, and according to his account he reached China.

10
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:10,025
He also traveled by sea along the coast of East Africa.

11
00:01:10,025 --> 00:01:15,800
Some historians discuss parts of his story, but his main route is well known.

12
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:30,600
Historians estimate that he traveled around one hundred and twenty thousand kilometers in almost thirty years, much more than famous European travelers of his time, and he visited the lands of dozens of today's countries.

13
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,366
Near the end he traveled again, crossing the Sahara to the kingdom of Mali around thirteen fifty two.

14
00:01:38,366 --> 00:01:44,200
Then he returned to Morocco, where he finally settled after decades on the road.

15
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:51,569
The Sultan of Morocco asked him to tell his story, and a writer named Ibn Juzayy wrote it down.

16
00:01:51,569 --> 00:02:01,400
The book, called the Rihla, was finished in thirteen fifty five and describes cities, customs, food and people across the known world.

17
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:07,109
Ibn Battuta teaches us curiosity, patience and respect for other cultures.

18
00:02:07,109 --> 00:02:15,000
One road is rarely enough to understand the world, and every journey begins with a first step out of the door.

19
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:17,093
Thank you for watching.

20
00:02:17,093 --> 00:02:20,920
New episodes of Jerry Documentaries are coming soon.

21
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:24,600
Tell us in the comments which topic you want next.

